var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"alg.html#Simulation-Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"Estimating the growth rate of a growing population of cells directly is difficult: since the number of cells will typically grow exponentially, obtaining reliable estimates has exponential complexity. Chemostats.jl bypasses this by using population control algorithms that only simulate a subset of the population, while still being able to extrapolate the expected number of cells with good accuracy.\n\nAll of these algorithms will terminate if the population dies out, as they cannot estimate growth rates in that case. In this case, simulations will have to be restarted. Chemostats.Thin and Chemostats.Lax both increase the probability of extinction, depending on parameter settings.\n\nChemostats.jl simulates cells independently of each other - direct cell-to-cell interactions are currently not supported. Except for Chemostats.Strict, all algorithms below support parallelisation.\n\n(Image: Illustration of various algorithms)","title":"Simulation Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"alg.html#Supported-algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"Supported algorithms"},{"category":"function","location":"alg.html#Chemostats.Direct","page":"Algorithms","text":"Direct()\n\nSimulates an entire population. The standard approach. Slow. If the population grows with rate  Λ, simulating it for time t will take e^Λt computational effort,  while providing accuracy that only scales as 1  t.\n\nNote: This algorithm is implemented as an alias of Thin(0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Direct"},{"category":"type","location":"alg.html#Chemostats.Thin","page":"Algorithms","text":"Thin(δ::Float64)\n\nSimulates a population where each cell is subject to a constant death rate δ  0.  If the original population grows with rate Λ, the simulated population grows with rate Λ - δ,  from which Λ can be estimated. \n\nFaster than Direct, but still slow. \n\nNote: This algorithm increases the chances that the population will die out.  To reduce the chances of this happening, increase the starting size of the population.\n\nThis algorithm requires δ  Λ to be stable, otherwise the population will eventually die out.  This approach has exponential complexity with rate 0  Λ - δ  Λ. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Thin"},{"category":"type","location":"alg.html#Chemostats.Strict","page":"Algorithms","text":"Strict(L::Int)\n\nImplements the strict chemostat, similar to the cloning algorithm of Giardinà et al. (2006) and Lecomte & Tailleur (2007).  Simulates a population of exactly L cells. When a cell divides in two, one of the resulting L+1 cells is discarded at random; when a cell dies, another is cloned into its place. \n\nSince the population size is kept constant, this algorithm has stable simulation times. Introduces an error of order  1  L into growth rate estimates. For many problems, L = 10-100 should be enough to obtain good estimates. For L geq 100 we recommend Lax instead.\n\nIf clone = false, dead cells are not replaced by clones, so the population can shrink below L. \n\nNote: This algorithm does not support parallelisation.\n\nNote 2: Lineage recording currently does not support clones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Strict"},{"category":"type","location":"alg.html#Chemostats.Lax","page":"Algorithms","text":"Lax(L::Int, τ::Float64; tol = 2, β = 0.5, L_min = 25)\n\nImplements the relaxed chemostat algorithm, combining Thin and Strict. The population is kept around L by introducing a time-varying death rate δ that is adapted to the current growth rate. The death rate is updated at intervals of length τ.\n\nThis algorithm trades the strict population size guarantees of Strict for better parallelisation. As the population size is only approximately L, runtimes may be somewhat less stable than Strict. In particular, if the population size hits 0 within an interval τ, the population dies out. For this reason, the algorithm sets δ = 0 when the population drops below L_min. To reduce the chances of this happening, increase L or decrease τ. We recommend L geq 50-100.\n\nThis algorithm determines δ on the fly by estimating the instantaneous growth rate of the population as \n\nhat Λ_n = frac1 - β1 - β^n sum_i=0^n-1 β^i hat Lambda(t_n-i-1 t_n-i)\n\nand requiring the expected population size after the next interval to equal L. Here\n\nhat Lambda(t_n-i-1 t_n-i) = fraclog hat N(t_n-i) - log hat N(t_n-i-1)tau\n\nis the growth rate in the n-ith interval. The parameter 0 leq β leq 1 controls smoothing: Larger β produces more stable estimates, but does not capture sudden variations in growth rate, whereas smaller β produces less stable estimates that can better adapt to fast fluctuations. If β = 0, only the growth rate in the last interval is used. The default is beta = 05.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Lax"},{"category":"type","location":"alg.html#Chemostats.Forward","page":"Algorithms","text":"Forward(L::Int)\n\nSimulated L independent lineages. When a cell divides, it is replaced by one of its daughter cells. Does not currently support cell death. This algorithm is not equivalent to Strict, except in the case L = 1.\n\nNote: This algorithm does not allow estimation of growth rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Forward"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/growth_protein.html#Protein-Dependent-Growth","page":"Protein-dependent growth","text":"This model is inspired by Piho & Thomas, \"Feedback between stochastic gene networks and population dynamics enables cellular decision-making\", Sci. Adv. (2024).\n\nA gene G switches on and off at random, and while on, it produces a protein P in bursts. The protein P in turn increases the growth rate of the cell, which causes cell-to-cell variability in growth rates. We can implement this in Catalyst.jl as follows:\n\nusing Catalyst\nusing Distributions\nimport Distributions: Geometric\n\n@register_symbolic Geometric(p)     # allows random burst sizes in reactions\n@parameters b\nburst_size = rand(Geometric(1 / (1 + b)))\n\nrn = @reaction_network begin\n    @species G_on(t) G_off(t) P(t)\n    @variables R(t)                       # time to division\n    @parameters k_on k_off f K n r_max\n\n    k_on, G_off --> G_on\n    k_off, G_on --> G_off\n    f, G_on --> G_on + $burst_size * P    # protein production\n\n    @equations begin\n        D(R) ~ -(1 + hill(P, r_max - 1, K, n))\n    end\nend\n\np = [ :k_on => 0.05, :k_off => 0.05,      # gene switching rates\n      :f => 10.0, :b => 4.0,              # burst frequency while the gene is on, and mean burst size\n      :K => 30.0,                         # protein number at half-maximal growth rate\n      :n => 4,                            # Hill coefficient of the growth rate\n      :r_max => 2.0 ]                     # maximal growth rate\n\ncycle = Gamma(5, 1.0);                    # cell cycle duration in the absence of protein","title":"Protein-Dependent Growth"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/growth_protein.html#Division","page":"Protein-dependent growth","text":"A cell divides once its remaining lifetime R reaches 0. When it divides, proteins are partitioned binomially between the two daughters.\n\nusing Chemostats\nusing Chemostats: Offspring, BinomialPartition\n\ncb_div = Chemostats.DivideCallback(rn.R ~ 0)\n\ndivide_cell = Offspring(2, :P => BinomialPartition(), :R => cycle);\n\nWe tell Chemostats to record the average protein content by overriding Chemostats.get_data:\n\nusing StatsBase: ProbabilityWeights\n\nfunction Chemostats.get_data(cell::DECell{I,typeof(divide_cell)}) where {I}\n    sol = cell.int.sol\n    mean(sol[:P][1:end-1], ProbabilityWeights(diff(sol.t)))\nend;\n\nWe create a HybridProblem to simulate the cells. The remaining time R follows an ODE, and the gene state and protein number changes by jumps.\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5\nusing JumpProcesses\n\nu0 = [ :G_on => 1, :G_off => 0, :P => 30, :R => mean(cycle) ]\nprob = HybridProblem(rn, u0, (0., Inf), p; callback = cb_div)\n\ncreate_population(L) = [ DECell(prob, Tsit5(), divide_cell) for _ in 1:L ];","title":"Division"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/growth_protein.html#Simulating-populations","page":"Protein-dependent growth","text":"We first simulate L individual lineages using Chemostats.Forward, and then a population of L competing lineages using Chemostats.Strict. In both cases we record the entire lineage tree with save_mode = :full.\n\nL = 50\ntmax = 1000.0\n\nchem_fwd = Chemostats.simulate(create_population(L), tmax, Chemostats.Forward(L); save_mode = :full)\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(create_population(L), tmax, Chemostats.Strict(L); save_mode = :full);\n\nThe data we stored for each node is the average protein content of the cell. We obtain a backward lineage by tracing the ancestors of a living cell.\n\nmeans_fwd = [ node.data for node in values(chem_fwd.tree) ]\nmeans_pop = [ node.data for node in values(chem.tree) ]\nmeans_bwd = [ node.data for node in Chemostats.ancestors(chem.tree, first(chem.pop)) ]\n\n(forward = mean(means_fwd), population = mean(means_pop), backward = mean(means_bwd))\n\n(forward = 94.13107324560731, population = 108.84294479127614, backward = 126.42998522092203)\n\nWe see that forward, population and backward statistics are all different. Cells that produce more protein divide faster, and are thus overrepresented in the population and in ancestral lineages.\n\nWe next compare cell lifetimes with average protein content. Here we ignore cells that are currently alive and have not yet divided.\n\ndivided = filter(!Chemostats.is_alive, chem_fwd.tree)\nlifetimes = [ Chemostats.lifetime(node) for node in divided ]\nmeans_div = [ node.data for node in divided ];","title":"Simulating populations"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/growth_protein.html#Results","page":"Protein-dependent growth","text":"<details><summary>Plotting code</summary>\n\nusing CairoMakie\nset_theme!(theme_minimal())\n\nblue, orange = colorant\"#85b5fb\", colorant\"#e07700\"\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"Average protein content\", ylabel = \"Density\", title = \"Protein-dependent growth\")\n\nbins = range(0, maximum(vcat(means_fwd, means_pop, means_bwd)); length = 40)\nfor (means, label, color) in [ (means_fwd, \"Forward\", blue), (means_pop, \"Population\", :black),\n                               (means_bwd, \"Backward\", orange) ]\n    stephist!(ax, means; bins, normalization = :pdf, label, color, linewidth = 2)\n    vlines!(ax, [ mean(means) ]; color, linestyle = :dash)\nend\naxislegend(ax)\n\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xlabel = \"Average protein content\", ylabel = \"Lifetime\", title = \"Forward lineages\")\nscatter!(ax2, means_div, lifetimes; color = (blue, 0.3), markersize = 4);\n\n</details>\n\n(Image: )","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/growth_protein.html#A-lineage-tree","page":"Protein-dependent growth","text":"We can visualise the resulting lineage trees using BasicTreePlots.jl. Chemostats can export trees in Newick format, which can be read by many tree-handling packages.\n\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(create_population(1), 20.0, Chemostats.Strict(L, false); save_mode = :full)\n\ntree = chem.tree\nroot = only(keys(Chemostats.roots(tree)))\ntspan = (0, 10.)\nfirst_cells = Chemostats.Trees.alive_during(tree, tspan)\n\nTreeView(27 nodes)\n\n<details><summary>Plotting code</summary>\n\nusing BasicTreePlots\nusing NewickTree: readnw\n\n# NewickTree ignores the length of the root branch, so we add a parent above the root\nnwtree = readnw(\"(\" * chop(strip(Chemostats.Trees.newick(first_cells))) * \");\")\n\n# `descendants` lists the same cells in preorder, like the Newick tree\nprotein = [ node.data for node in Chemostats.descendants(tree, root; self = true, tspan) ]\n\ncolorrange = (0, maximum(protein))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 450))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"Time\", title = \"Lineage tree from a single cell ($(length(protein)) cells)\")\nhideydecorations!(ax)\nhidespines!(ax, :l, :r, :t)\n\ntp = treeplot!(ax, nwtree; branchcolor = [ protein[1]; protein ], branchwidth = 3, colormap = :Oranges, colorrange)\ntranslate!(tp, tspan[1], 0, 0)      # the plotted tree starts at 0, at the birth of the root\nxlims!(ax, tspan...)                # cells alive at the end of `tspan` are cut off\nColorbar(fig[1, 2]; colormap = :Oranges, limits = colorrange, label = \"Average protein content\");\n\n</details>\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"A lineage tree"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/analysis.html#Lineage-analysis","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"We use a small random tree in which the data of each cell is a random number.\n\nusing Chemostats.Trees\nusing Chemostats.Trees: cell_init!, cell_divide!, cell_die!, cell_update!\nusing Random\nusing StatsBase\n\nmutable struct Cell end\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\ntree = Tree{Cell,Float64}()\ncells = [ Cell() for _ in 1:20 ]\ncell_init!(tree, cells, 0.)\n\nfor t in 0.1:0.1:20\n    i = rand(eachindex(cells))\n    c = cells[i]\n    kids = [ Cell(), Cell() ]\n    cell_divide!(tree, c, kids, t, rand())\n    cells[i] = kids[1]\n\n    # Keep the population size constant by replacing a random cell\n    j = rand(eachindex(cells))\n    cell_die!(tree, cells[j], t, rand())\n    cells[j] = kids[2]\nend\n\nforeach(c -> cell_update!(tree, c, 20., rand()), cells)\nsort!(tree)\nTree{Main.Cell}(410 nodes, 20 living)","title":"Lineage analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/analysis.html#Selecting-cells","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"Most analysis functions take arbitrary node collections, which can be:\n\na tree, using values(tree)\na subtree view(tree, ids)[@ref Base.view(::Tree, ::Any)]\ncells satisfying certain conditions, using filter(f, nodes)\nancestors of a cell using ancestors(tree, id)\ndescendants of a cell using descendants(tree, id)\n\nFor these collections, keys returns the cell ids, values the nodes, and pairs returns id => node.\n\nid = first(keys(living(tree)))\nlineage = ancestors(tree, id; self=true, tspan=(5., 20.))\nlate = filter(node -> node.t_birth >= 5, tree)\nlength(collect(lineage)), length(late)\n(18, 293)","title":"Selecting cells"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/analysis.html#Observables-and-summaries","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"Trees uses two kinds of user-defined functions:\n\nObservables f(node, a) of a cell at age a (time since birth). This could be a cell's position, size, protein content etc.\nPer-cell summaries f(node) of a Node, e.g. its lifetime, or the time average of an observable using time_average(f, node). ","title":"Observables and summaries"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/analysis.html#Population-and-lineage-statistics","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"mean, var and mean_and_var are defined for trees and lineages. To compute time-averages, use time_weights to weight cells by their lifetime.\n\nage(node, a) = a\navg_age = time_average(age)\n\nmean(avg_age, late),                                    # per cell\nmean(avg_age, tree, time_weights),         # time-weighted, over the population\nmean(avg_age, lineage, time_weights)    # time-weighted, along a lineage\n(0.4792096219931269, 0.948125, 0.5606666666666666)","title":"Population and lineage statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/analysis.html#Snapshots","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"snapshot(f, nodes, t) evaluates an observable over all cells alive at time t. t can be an array, in which case multiple snapshots are pooled.\n\n[ mean(snapshot(age, tree, t)) for t in (10., 15.) ]\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.9349999999999998\n 1.01\n\nSnapshots and age-conditioned records return vectors of values. Observables can return several values, e.g. to pair the birth and division times of each cell:\n\ndiv = at_division((node, τ) -> (node.t_birth, node.t_birth + τ), late)     # birth and division times\n142-element Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}:\n (5.0, 5.7)\n (5.1, 5.4)\n (5.2, 5.8)\n (5.3, 6.1)\n (5.4, 9.7)\n (5.5, 6.0)\n (5.6, 6.2)\n (5.6, 6.6)\n (5.7, 6.3)\n (5.8, 7.0)\n ⋮\n (17.9, 19.0)\n (18.0, 19.6)\n (18.1, 18.7)\n (18.2, 19.2)\n (18.6, 18.8)\n (19.1, 19.5)\n (19.3, 20.0)\n (19.5, 19.7)\n (19.5, 19.8)","title":"Snapshots"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/analysis.html#Base.view-Tuple{Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Any}","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"view(tree::Tree, ids)\n\nReturns the nodes ids of tree, without copying.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.view"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/analysis.html#Base.filter-Tuple{Any, Union{Chemostats.Trees.AncestorIterator, Chemostats.Trees.DescendantIterator, Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Chemostats.Trees.TreeView}}","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"filter(f, nodes)\n\nReturns the nodes of nodes for which f(node) is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.filter"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.ancestors","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"ancestors(tree::Tree, id; self=false, tspan = (-Inf, Inf))\nancestors(tree::Tree, cell; self=false, tspan = (-Inf, Inf))\n\nIterates over the ancestors (nodes) of node id (its backward lineage), starting with its parent. If self=true, start with the cell itself. Only cells alive at some time in tspan are included. cell must be a living cell of the tree. Use keys for their ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.ancestors"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.descendants","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"descendants(tree::Tree, id; self=false, tspan = (-Inf, Inf))\n\nIterates over the descendants (nodes) of node id (its forward lineages) in depth-first manner. If self=true, start with the cell itself. Only cells alive at some time in tspan are included. Requires a sorted tree. Use keys for their ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.descendants"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.roots","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"roots(tree::Tree)\n\nReturns all root nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.roots"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.living","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"living(tree::Tree)\n\nReturns all living cells, as a node collection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.living"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.children","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"children(tree::Tree, id)\n\nReturns the children of node id as a node collection. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.children"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.alive_at","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"alive_at(tree::Tree, t)\nalive_at(nodes, t)\n\nReturns the nodes in nodes alive at time t. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\nalive_at(nodes, t)\n\nReturns nodes alive at time t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.alive_at"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.alive_during","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"alive_during(nodes, tspan)\n\nReturns nodes alive at some time in tspan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.alive_during"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.time_average","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"time_average(f, node::Node; tspan = (-Inf, Inf), integrate)\ntime_average(f; tspan = (-Inf, Inf), integrate)\n\nReturns the time average of f(node, age) over the cell's lifetime, truncated to lie within tspan. Returns nothing for nodes without data, or for cells not alive within tspan.\n\ntime_average(f; kwargs...) is a convenience wrapper for node -> time_average(f, node; kwargs...).\n\nTime averages are computed by integration, using the callback integrate(f, node, a, b). By default, this uses adaptive Gaussian quadrature (QuadGK.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.time_average"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.time_weights","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"time_weights(node::Node; tspan = (-Inf, Inf))\ntime_weights(; tspan = (-Inf, Inf))\n\nWeights cells by their lifetime within tspan (living cells up to their last update). Used to compute time averages in lineages and populations. time_weights(; tspan) returns the function node -> time_weights(node; tspan).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.time_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/analysis.html#Statistics.mean-Tuple{Any, Union{Chemostats.Trees.AncestorIterator, Chemostats.Trees.DescendantIterator, Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Chemostats.Trees.TreeView}, Any}","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"mean(f, nodes)\nmean(f, nodes, w)\n\nReturns the mean of f(node) over all nodes in nodes, optionally weighted by w. The default returns the average over cells; if w = time_weights, returns time averages. Ignores nodes where f returns nothing.\n\nFor example:\n\n# Time average of an observable `f(node, age)` over the population within `tspan`\nmean(time_average(f; tspan), tree, time_weights(; tspan))\n\n# Mean lifetime of the cells along the backward lineage of node `id`\nmean(lifetime, ancestors(tree, id))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.mean"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/analysis.html#Statistics.var-Tuple{Any, Union{Chemostats.Trees.AncestorIterator, Chemostats.Trees.DescendantIterator, Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Chemostats.Trees.TreeView}, Any}","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"var(f, nodes; corrected=true)\nvar(f, nodes, w; corrected=true)\n\nReturns the variance of f(node) over all nodes in nodes, optionally weighted by w, see mean. If corrected=true, applies Bessel's correction (for probability weights).\n\nNote: This is the variance of per-cell summaries. The variance of per-cell averages is smaller than the variance across the population (law of total variance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.var"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/analysis.html#StatsBase.mean_and_var-Tuple{Any, Union{Chemostats.Trees.AncestorIterator, Chemostats.Trees.DescendantIterator, Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Chemostats.Trees.TreeView}, Any}","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"mean_and_var(f, nodes; corrected=true)\nmean_and_var(f, nodes, w; corrected=true)\n\nReturns the mean and variance of f(node) over all nodes in nodes in a single pass, optionally weighted by w, see mean and var. If corrected=true, applies Bessel's correction to the variance (for probability weights).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsBase.mean_and_var"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.snapshot","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"snapshot(f, nodes, t)\n\nReturns f(node, age) for all nodes in nodes alive at time t. Nodes without data are skipped. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\nsnapshot(f, nodes, ts::AbstractVector)\n\nReturns the concatenated snapshots of the nodes in nodes at the times ts. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.at_age","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"at_age(f, nodes, a)\n\nReturns f(node, a) for the nodes in nodes that have reached age a. Nodes without data, or nodes that do not reach age a, are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.at_age"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.at_birth","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"at_birth(f, nodes)\n\nReturns f(node, 0) for the nodes of the node collection nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.at_birth"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/analysis.html#Chemostats.Trees.at_division","page":"Lineage analysis","text":"at_division(f, nodes)\n\nReturns f(node, τ) for the nodes of the node collection nodes that divided, where τ is their generation time. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.at_division"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/index.html#Lineage-trees","page":"Lineage trees","text":"Chemostats.Trees is a submodule for manipulating cell lineages and populations. The goal is to provide a simple, lightweight and flexible API for basic lineage analysis. ","title":"Lineage trees"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/index.html#Features","page":"Lineage trees","text":"Can store cells with any data format\nUtilities for iterating over ancestors and descendants\nStatistical functions to compute lineage and population averages\nAutomatic garbage collection (pruning) of trees","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/index.html#Example","page":"Lineage trees","text":"The following example constructs a small tree by hand, where each cell stores its birth volume. A cell divides once it has doubled in size, and its daughters inherit fractions f and 1 - f of its volume.\n\nusing Chemostats.Trees\nusing Chemostats.Trees: cell_init!, cell_divide!, cell_die!, cell_update!\nusing StatsBase\n\nmutable struct Cell\n    v_birth::Float64\nend\n\n# Divides `cell` at time `t` into daughters with fractions `f` and `1 - f` of its volume\nfunction divide!(tree, cell, t, f)\n    v_div = 2 * cell.v_birth\n    daughters = [ Cell(f * v_div), Cell((1 - f) * v_div) ]\n    cell_divide!(tree, cell, daughters, t, cell.v_birth)\n    daughters\nend\n\nr = Cell(1.)\ntree = Tree{Cell,Float64}()\ncell_init!(tree, [ r ], 0.)\na, b = divide!(tree, r, 1., 0.25)                # r divides at t = 1: a and b have volumes 0.5 and 1.5\na1, a2 = divide!(tree, a, 2., 0.5)               # a divides at t = 2: a1 and a2 have volume 0.5\nb1, b2 = divide!(tree, b, 2.5, 0.4)              # b divides at t = 2.5: b1 and b2 have volumes 1.2 and 1.8\ncell_die!(tree, a2, 2.5, a2.v_birth)             # a2 is removed at t = 2.5\nfor cell in (a1, b1, b2)                         # update living cells before analysing the tree\n    cell_update!(tree, cell, 3., cell.v_birth)\nend\nsort!(tree)\nTree{Main.Cell}(7 nodes, 3 living)\n\nNodes can be queried by id, which relates cells to each other in the tree:\n\nid = first(keys(living(tree)))      # 4 (a1)\ntree[id]                            # Node{Float64}(2.0 → 3.0, data = 0.5)\ncollect(keys(ancestors(tree, id)))  # [2, 1] (a, r)\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 2\n 1\n\nWe can extract population statistics of the form f(node, a), where a is the cell age. Here, cells grow exponentially and double their volume in one time unit, so the volume of a cell at age a is:\n\nvolume(node, a) = node.data * 2^a\nsnapshot(volume, tree, 1.5)                      # [0.707, 2.121], i.e. a and b\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7071067811865476\n 2.121320343559643\n\nWe can also compute population statistics such as time or per-cell averages, over entire populations, descendants, or ancestral lineages.\n\nmean(time_average(volume), tree, time_weights)                        # 1.489\nmean(time_average(volume), descendants(tree, 3; self=true), time_weights)  # 2.300 (b, b1, b2)\nmean(node -> node.data, ancestors(tree, id; self=true))              # 0.667 (a1, a, r)\n0.6666666666666666","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/index.html#See-also","page":"Lineage trees","text":"tskit, whose tables and simplify algorithm inspired the pruning in Trees.","title":"See also"},{"category":"module","location":"trees/index.html#Chemostats.Trees","page":"Lineage trees","text":"Trees\n\nFunctionality for representing cell population trees and lineages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/threshold_division.html#Threshold-Triggered-Division","page":"Threshold-triggered division","text":"This model is based on ElGamel & Mugler, \"Effects of molecular noise on cell size control\", Phys. Rev. Lett. (2024).\n\nCells grow exponentially and divide once a molecule x reaches a threshold. The molecule is produced at rate ν + μV, where V is the cell volume, and degraded at rate λ. Depending on these rates, this can result in cells that behave like timers, adders or sizers.","title":"Threshold-Triggered Division"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/threshold_division.html#The-model","page":"Threshold-triggered division","text":"using Catalyst\n\nrn = @reaction_network begin\n    @species x(t)\n    @variables V(t)\n    @parameters α ν μ λ N\n\n    ν + μ * V, 0 --> x\n    λ, x --> 0\n\n    @equations begin\n        D(V) ~ α * V\n    end\nend\n\nModel ##ReactionSystem#152:\nUnknowns (2): see unknowns(##ReactionSystem#152)\n  x(t)\n  V(t)\nParameters (5): see parameters(##ReactionSystem#152)\n  α\n  ν\n  μ\n  λ\n  N","title":"The model"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/threshold_division.html#Division","page":"Threshold-triggered division","text":"The cell divides once x reaches the threshold N. At division, the volume is halved and each daughter starts with N / 2 molecules.\n\nusing Chemostats\nusing Chemostats: Offspring, ExactPartition\n\nN = 200             # threshold\n\ncb_div = Chemostats.DivideCallback(rn.x ~ rn.N; discrete = true)\n\ndivide_cell = Offspring(2, :V => ExactPartition(), :x => N / 2);\n\nWe tell Chemostats to record the birth and division size of each cell by overriding Chemostats.get_data:\n\nfunction Chemostats.get_data(cell::DECell{I,typeof(divide_cell)}) where {I}\n    V = cell.int.sol[:V]\n    (V_b = V[1], V_d = V[end])\nend;\n\nWe create a HybridProblem to simulate the cells. The volume V follows an ODE, and x changes by jumps. VR_Direct assumes that time starts at 0, so each cell runs on its own clock (reset_t = true).\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5\nusing JumpProcesses\n\nfunction create_population(rates, L)\n    p = [ :α => 1.0, :ν => rates.ν, :μ => rates.μ, :λ => rates.λ, :N => N ]\n    u0 = [ :V => 1.0, :x => N / 2 ]\n\n    prob = HybridProblem(rn, u0, (0., Inf), p; callback = cb_div, vr_aggregator = VR_Direct(),\n                         save_positions = (false, false))\n\n    [ DECell(prob, Tsit5(), divide_cell; reset_t = true) for _ in 1:L ]\nend;","title":"Division"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/threshold_division.html#Simulation","page":"Threshold-triggered division","text":"We simulate L lineages using Chemostats.Forward for three choices of rates, and compute the noise in the birth size.\n\nusing StatsBase\n\nregimes = [ \"timer-like\" => (ν = 80.0, μ = 44.0, λ = 0.01),\n            \"adder-like\" => (ν = 2.0, μ = 98.0, λ = 0.01),\n            \"sizer-like\" => (ν = 2.0, μ = 1000.0, λ = 10.0) ]\n\nL = 50\ntmax = 100.0\nt_burn = 20.0\n\n# Birth and division sizes of the cells that divided after the burn-in\nfunction cell_sizes(rates)\n    chem = Chemostats.simulate(create_population(rates, L), tmax, Chemostats.Forward(L);\n                               save_mode = :full)\n\n    [ node.data for node in values(chem.tree) if node.t_birth >= t_burn && !node.alive ]\nend\n\nsizes = [ cell_sizes(rates) for (name, rates) in regimes ]\nbirths = [ [ s.V_b for s in sz ] for sz in sizes ]\nnoise = [ name => var(b) / mean(b)^2 for ((name, rates), b) in zip(regimes, births) ]\n\n3-element Vector{Pair{String, Float64}}:\n \"timer-like\" => 0.007455275408670122\n \"adder-like\" => 0.0033849408482365946\n \"sizer-like\" => 0.0041918366683019685","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/threshold_division.html#Results","page":"Threshold-triggered division","text":"<details><summary>Plotting code</summary>\n\nusing CairoMakie\nset_theme!(theme_minimal())\n\ncolors = [ colorant\"#85b5fb\", :gray, colorant\"#e07700\" ]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 350))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = \"Birth size\", ylabel = \"Added size\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xlabel = \"Birth size\", ylabel = \"Density\")\n\nfor ((name, rates), sz, color) in zip(regimes, sizes, colors)\n    V_b = [ s.V_b for s in sz ]\n    ΔV = [ s.V_d - s.V_b for s in sz ]\n    slope = cov(V_b, ΔV) / var(V_b)\n    xs = collect(extrema(V_b))\n\n    scatter!(ax, V_b, ΔV; color = (color, 0.2), markersize = 3, label = name => (; markersize = 8, color))\n    lines!(ax, xs, mean(ΔV) .+ slope .* (xs .- mean(V_b)); color, linewidth = 2)\n    stephist!(ax2, V_b; bins = 50, normalization = :pdf, color, linewidth = 2)\nend\naxislegend(ax; position = :rt);\n\n</details>\n\n(Image: )\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cells.html#Cells","page":"Cells","text":"Cells in Chemostats.jl can be of any user-defined type that implements the cell interface. For models defined using DifferentialEquations.jl, the DECell class provides a direct wrapper.","title":"Cells"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cells.html#decell-page","page":"Cells","text":"DECell also supports models defined using the wider SciML ecosystem, e.g. using ModelingToolkit.jl and Catalyst.jl.\n\nTo use this functionality we require:\n\nAn AbstractDEProblem describing how the cells behave\nA DivideCallback provided to the AbstractDEProblem\nA divide function\n\nChemostats.jl supports AbstractDEProblems with arbitrary parameters p and state vectors u. This problem should include a DivideCallback to determine when a cell has reached the end of its life, either due to division or death. When a cell reaches the end of its life, the divide function is called to determine the daughter cells. \n\nThe divide argument should be a function of the form int -> offspring that takes a single argument int, the integrator of the cell, and returns an Offspring object describing the daughter cells. This function can return nothing if there are no offspring. Alternatively, a fixed Offspring object can be passed directly.\n\n# When the cell divides, the volume is halved, ribosomes are partitioned binomially,\n# and each daughter draws a new cell cycle duration\nOffspring(2, :V => ExactPartition(), :R => BinomialPartition(), :τ => Gamma(5, 1))\n\n# Alternative: die with probability 1/10, otherwise divide asymmetrically\nfunction divide(int)\n    rand() < 0.1 && return nothing\n\n    f = rand(Beta(20, 20))\n    Offspring(:V => ExactPartition([f, 1 - f]), :R => BinomialPartition([f, 1 - f]))\nend\n\nDeprecated: divide can return a vector of NamedTuples with fields u0 and p, the initial state and parameters of each daughter cell. This is needed to change parameters without symbolic names, e.g. a NamedTuple of parameters.","title":"DifferentialEquations.jl models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/cells.html#cell-interface","page":"Cells","text":"Cells of a user-defined type must implement the following functions.","title":"Custom cells"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.DECell","page":"Cells","text":"DECell(prob, alg, divide; t0 = 0., reset_t=false, remake::Bool=false, rng=Xoshiro(rand(UInt64)))\n\nCreates a new cell around a SciMLBase.DEProblem, simulated with algorithm alg starting at time t0. The function divide takes a single integrator argument int and returns a list of offspring as a vector of NamedTuples with fields u0 and p.\n\nreset_t determines whether the integration time is started from 0 for every cell, which can avoid floating-point errors for long simulations (many generations). If reset_t is set to true, each cell should keep track of its starting time, e.g. via a parameter t0 in p.\n\nIf remake is true, descendants will be created using SciMLBase.remake, which creates  a copy of the underlying AbstractProblem (safest). Otherwise, descendants will be  created using SciMLBase.reinit, which should be faster for ODEProblem and SDEProblem types.\n\nIf a population tree is recorded, the solution int.sol of each cell is saved for each cell. This can be changed by overriding Chemostats.get_data.\n\nNote: The problem should include a callback that calls terminate! when a cell reaches the end of its life.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.DECell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.DivideCallback","page":"Cells","text":"DivideCallback(condition; discrete=false, kwargs...)\n\nImplements a differential equation callback to check whether a cell has reached the end of its lifetime. If discrete=false (default), returns a ContinuousCallback that calls terminate! once condition  equals zero. If discrete=true, returns a DiscreteCallback.  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.DivideCallback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.Offspring","page":"Cells","text":"Offspring([n,] rules...)\n\nDescribes initial conditions and parameters for the n offspring of a dividing cell. Each rule is of the form var => value, where value can be one of the following:\n\nExactPartition(f): var is divided exactly among the offspring\nBinomialPartition(f): var is partitioned binomially among the offspring\nAllOrNothingPartition(f): var goes entirely to one offspring\n[ val1, val2, ..., valn ]: the i-th offspring gets the i-th value\nval: each offspring gets val,\ndist::Distribution: each offspring gets an independent random sample from dist\n\nVariables are given as symbols, or a CartesianIndex. All other variables are copied from the parent.\n\nAn Offspring can be used as the divide keyword of a DECell. Alternatively, divide can be a function int -> offspring that returns an Offspring object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Offspring"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.ExactPartition","page":"Cells","text":"ExactPartition(f = nothing)\n\nDivides x among all offspring, such that offspring i receives f[i] * x. The fractions f must be nonnegative and sum to 1. By default, x is divided equally. \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.ExactPartition"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.BinomialPartition","page":"Cells","text":"BinomialPartition(f = nothing)\n\nDivides a count x (rounded to an integer) among the offspring of a cell, following  a multinomial distribution. Each molecule is independently assigned to offspring i  with probability f[i]. The fractions f must be nonnegative and sum to 1.  By default, all offspring are equally likely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.BinomialPartition"},{"category":"type","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.AllOrNothingPartition","page":"Cells","text":"AllOrNothingPartition(f = nothing)\n\nAssigns all of a quantity x to a single offspring, chosen at random with probabilities f. The probabilities f must be nonnegative and sum to 1. By default, all offspring are equally likely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.AllOrNothingPartition"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.get_curr_t","page":"Cells","text":"get_curr_t(cell)\n\nReturns the current simulation time t of the cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.get_curr_t"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.get_state","page":"Cells","text":"get_state(cell)\n\nReturns the current cell state, see CellState.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.get_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.divide!","page":"Cells","text":"divide!(cell)\n\nCalled after a cell divides, as determined by Chemostats.get_children. After this call, Chemostats.get_state should return Divided.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.divide!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.die!","page":"Cells","text":"die!(cell)\n\nCalled after a cell dies, as determined by Chemostats.get_children. After this call, Chemostats.get_state should return Dead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.die!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.kill!","page":"Cells","text":"kill!(cell, t)\n\nCalled when a cell is killed by the simulation algorithm. The argument t is the time at which a cell is killed. Unless the cell is already dead, Chemostats.get_state should return Killed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.kill!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.get_children","page":"Cells","text":"get_children(parent, args...)\n\nReturns an array of cells representing the offspring of the given cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.get_children"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.clone_cell","page":"Cells","text":"clone_cell(cell, t)\n\nCreates an independent copy of the cell at time t. Used by Chemostats.Strict to maintain a constant population size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.clone_cell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.get_cell_rng","page":"Cells","text":"get_cell_rng(cell)\n\nReturns the random number generator owned by cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.get_cell_rng"},{"category":"function","location":"api/cells.html#Chemostats.get_data","page":"Cells","text":"get_data(cell)\n\nReturns the data saved for cell in the population tree. Defaults to nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.get_data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats","page":"Chemostats","text":"","title":"Chemostats"},{"category":"section","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostat-objects","page":"Chemostats","text":"Chemostats.jl simulates individual cells in a Chemostat object.","title":"Chemostat objects"},{"category":"section","location":"api/chemostats.html#Estimating-population-sizes","page":"Chemostats","text":"","title":"Estimating population sizes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.Chemostat","page":"Chemostats","text":"struct Chemostat{C,P,T}\n    pop::Vector{C}\n    p::P\n    snaps::Vector{Snapshot}\n    tree::T\n    prune::Bool\n    status::ReturnCode.T\nend\n\nChemostat object. The field pop contains the current population of cells. The field p can be a user-defined parameter object (defaults to nothing). snaps contains a list of Snapshots saved at different times. tree contains the population tree, depending on save_mode: :none (default) records no tree, :ancestors keeps the ancestors of the living cells and of the populations at the saveat times of simulate!, and :full keeps every cell. prune is true if the tree is pruned during simulations (save_mode = :ancestors). status contains the status code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Chemostat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.simulate","page":"Chemostats","text":"simulate(cells, tmax, alg, ensalg = EnsembleThreads(); kwargs...)\n\nUtility function that creates a Chemostat with initial population cells and runs simulate!. Returns the created Chemostat object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.simulate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.simulate!","page":"Chemostats","text":"simulate!(chem, tmax, alg, ensalg = EnsembleThreads(); saveat = [], Nmax = 1e7, throw_on_error = false)\n\nSimulates the chemostat until time tmax with algorithm alg (see Simulation Algorithms for a list of algorithms). ensalg can be EnsembleSerial() for single-threaded simulations, or EnsembleThreads() for multithreading (if alg support multithreading). The default ensalg is EnsembleThreads() for multithreaded algorithms, or EnsembleSerial() for single-threaded algorithms.\n\nIf the population size exceeds Nmax, the simulation exits (useful for Direct and Lax).\n\nIf a population tree is recorded, cells alive at times saveat are saved together with their  ancestors.\n\nIf throw_on_error is true, exceptions that occur while simulating a cell will stop the simulation.  If it is false, the exception will be caught and the cell will be removed from the population.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.simulate!"},{"category":"module","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.CellState","page":"Chemostats","text":"@enumx CellState\n\nThe current state of a cell as returned by Chemostats.get_state.\n\nNewborn: Newborn cell that has  not been simulated yet.\nAlive: Cell that is currently being simulated, but has not reached its end of life.\nEndOfLife: Cell that has reached its end of life, before its offspring are determined.\nDead: Cell that has died, leaving no offspring.\nDivided: Cell that has divided into daughter cells.\nKilled: Cell that has been killed (e.g. removed), by the Simulation Algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.CellState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.CellException","page":"Chemostats","text":"struct CellException <: Exception\n\nWrapper type for exceptions that occur during cell simulations\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.CellException"},{"category":"type","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.Snapshot","page":"Chemostats","text":"struct Snapshot\n    t::Float64\n    N::Int \n    nsim::Int\n    log_f::Float64\nend\n\nSaves the state of a simulated population at a fixed time t. Here N is the observed size of the population, and log_f is minus the log fraction of the true population observed. This allows us to use subsampling algorithms (see Simulation Algorithms) that only track a small subset of a full population and extrapolate.\n\nThe true population size can be estimated using est_logN or est_N. The field nsim  counts the number of cells simulated until the current snapshot (including partially simulated and removed cells).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.est_Λ","page":"Chemostats","text":"est_Λ(snap1::Snapshot, snap2::Snapshot)\n\nEstimates the growth rates from two snapshots at times t_1 and t_2 via \n\nhat Lambda(t_1 t_2) = fraclog hat N(t_2) - log hat N(t_1)t_2 - t_1\n\nHere hat N(t_1) and hat N(t_2) are estimated via est_N.\n\n\n\n\n\nest_Λ(snaps::AbstractVector{Snapshot}, t1 = first(snaps).t, t2)\n\nEstimates the growth rate of a population between times t1 and t2 from the snapshots saved at these times, see est_Λ. Errors if no snapshots were saved at these times. snaps can alternatively be a Chemostat instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.est_Λ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.est_N","page":"Chemostats","text":"est_N(snap::Snapshot)\n\nEstimates the true population size from a snapshot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.est_N"},{"category":"function","location":"api/chemostats.html#Chemostats.est_logN","page":"Chemostats","text":"est_logN(snap::Snapshot)\n\nEstimates the true log-population size from a snapshot.\n\n\n\n\n\nest_logN(snaps::AbstractVector{Snapshot}, t)\n\nEstimates the log-size of a population at time t from the snapshot saved at time t, see est_logN. Errors if no snapshot was saved at time t (see saveat in simulate!). snaps can alternatively be a Chemostat instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.est_logN"},{"category":"section","location":"theory.html#Theory","page":"Theory","text":"The main goal of Chemostats.jl is to efficiently the behaviour cell populations, with a focus on growth rates. The full theory behind the package is explained in the paper\n\nKaan Öcal, Lars Maas, Michael P.H. Stumpf. \"Probing bacterial fitness in virtual chemostats.\" bioRxiv (2026).\n\nThis section provides a brief overview of the basic idea. Given sufficient resources, a population of cells will grow exponentially in number as\n\nN(t) sim e^Lambda t\n\nwhere N is the number of cells, t is time and Lambda is the growth rate (which depends on the strain and the environment). For single-celled organisms such as bacteria, the growth rate is commonly used as a measure of evolutionary fitness: bacteria with a higher growth rate will outcompete others. Growth rates can also be used to estimate the selection coefficient s in population genetics. \n\nEstimating the growth rate from simulations is tricky. The naïve approach simulates a population for a time t, counts the number of cells N(t) and estimates the growth rate as \n\nhat Lambda = fraclog N(t)t\n\nThis does not scale well – the number of cells we need to simulate grows exponentially in t, whereas the accuracy of the growth rate estimate only decreases as 1t. This algorithm is implemented in Chemostats.jl via Chemostats.Direct().\n\nA common alternative is to estimate the growth rate Lambda by the mean generation time overlinetau as\n\nhat Lambda = fraclog(2)overline tau\n\nWe can do this by simulating a single lineage of cells, picking a random daughter cell at each division and estimating the long-term average of tau. This is implemented in Chemostats.jl via Chemostats.Strict(1). Unfortunately, this is only accurate if generation times do not vary: otherwise, it systematically underestimates the true growth rate [1]. This approach also does not take into account cell death – what happens when the tracked cell dies?\n\nIn general, the mean doubling time is less than the mean generation length. The reason is that population growth is exponential, not linear, and cells that divide faster than others disproportionately affect the growth rate. \n\nSimulating an entire cell population is too slow; simulating a single lineage is not accurate. Chemostats.jl gives the best of both worlds by simulating some, but not all cells in a population, using the algorithms Chemostats.Lax and Chemostats.Strict. The accuracy is controlled by the chemostat size L, which sets how many cells we simulate at once. Large L yields more accurate results, at the expense of longer simulation times. As shown in [2], the typical error decreases as 1  sqrtL, whereas the runtime increases simply as L. This means we can estimate growth rates accurately in (amortised) quadratic time instead of exponential! In practice, setting L = 10 or 100 should be sufficient to get good growth rate estimates.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"theory.html#Example:-Linear-Birth-Process","page":"Theory","text":"Let us see this in action by considering a very simple model of cell division: every cell lives for an exponentially distributed amount of time tau sim mathrmExp(1). This is the linear birth process, also known as the Yule process. This population grows with rate Lambda = 1. \n\nWe can implement this in Julia as follows (see demo/yule.jl):\n\nusing Chemostats\nusing Distributions\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5\nusing Random\n\n# We represent each cell by a single variable `u`, the time to division.\n# Then `u` follows a very simple ODE:\nf(u, p, t) = -one(u)\n\n# We divide when the time to division hits zero\ndiv_cond(u, t, int) = u\ncb_div = Chemostats.DivideCallback(div_cond)\n\n# The time to division of every cell is exponentially distributed\nu0 = randexp()\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f, u0, (0., Inf); callback=cb_div)\n\n# Two daughters with exponentially distributed times to division\ndivide = Chemostats.Offspring(2, CartesianIndex() => Exponential())\n\ncells = [ DECell(prob, Tsit5(), divide) ]\n\n# Estimate the growth rate from a single lineage. The true growth rate is\n# exactly 1, but tracking only one lineage systematically underestimates it.\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(cells, 1_000_000, Chemostats.Strict(1))\nprintln(\"Estimated growth rate: \", Chemostats.est_Λ(chem))\n\n\n\n\nEven though we simulate roughly a million generations, our estimate of the growth rate (0.692) is off by 30%. \n\nWe can investigate the estimation error depending on the Chemostat size L and the simulation time. The plot below shows the result for various choices of L and the Chemostats.Strict algorithm, described e.g. in [3,4]. The y-axis shows the root mean square error (RMSE) over 10 runs, and the x-axis measures computational effort by the number of cells simulated. For each value of L, we simulate the system for times t = 1 10 100 ldots. Note that both axes are logarithmic.\n\n(Image: )\n\nWe see that longer simulations times typically result in more accurate estimates, up to a point [1]. Beyond that, our estimates do not change, no matter how many cells we simulate. However, by increasing the chemostat size L, we get increasingly more accurate estimates. This is verified in the plot below, which shows the long-term error as a function of L:\n\n(Image: )\n\nHere the dashed line represents 1 / L, which matches the asymptotic error as predicted in [2]. Note in the above plot that the time it takes to reach this optimum grows as L, which can be seen in the first plot; the total computational effort is therefore approximately O(L cdot t) = O(L^2).","title":"Example: Linear Birth Process"},{"category":"section","location":"theory.html#Example:-Cell-size-control","page":"Theory","text":"Here we consider a simple model of cell size control [5] where each cell grows exponentially in size with rate 1 and divides once it hits a threshold size. The threshold size equals the birth size V_b plus a random amount Delta sim Gamma(5 02). When a cell divides, its daughters inherit a random fraction f of the parent's volume, where f sim Beta(1 1). \n\nWe can implement this in Julia as follows (see demo/sizecontrol    .jl):\n\nusing Chemostats\nusing Distributions\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5\n\n# We represent each cell by its size `u`, which grows exponentially in time:\nf(u, p, t) = u\n\n# We divide once a cell hits its division volume `V_d`, stored as a parameter\ndiv_cond(u, t, int) = u - int.p.V_d\ncb_div = Chemostats.DivideCallback(div_cond)\n\n# The division threshold is the birth volume plus a Gamma-distributed amount;\n# daughters inherit a random fraction of the parent's volume.\ndist_Δ = Gamma(5, 0.2)\ndist_div = Beta(1)\n\nu0 = 1.\np = (V_d = 2.,)\n\nprob = ODEProblem(f, u0, (0., Inf), p; callback=cb_div)\n\nfunction divide(int)\n    # Sample sizes of each daughter cell\n    f = rand(dist_div)\n    V1 = f * int.u\n    V2 = (1 - f) * int.u\n\n    # Return two cells with the given starting sizes and randomly sampled\n    # division thresholds\n    [ (u0 = V1, p = (V_d = V1 + rand(dist_Δ),)),\n      (u0 = V2, p = (V_d = V2 + rand(dist_Δ),)) ]\nend\n\ncells = [ DECell(prob, Tsit5(), divide) ]\n\n# The true growth rate equals the physical growth rate of each cell, Λ = 1.\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(cells, 1000., Chemostats.Strict(10))\nprintln(\"Estimated growth rate: \", Chemostats.est_Λ(chem))\n\n\n\n\nHere again the true growth rate of the population is Lambda = 1 – this agrees with the physical growth rate of each cell. Numerically estimating the growth rate yields the same picture as before:\n\n(Image: )\n\nAgain our growth rate estimates are only good up to a point, unless we increase the chemostat size L. The best possible error again decreases as 1L as shown in the plot below:\n\n(Image: )","title":"Example: Cell size control"},{"category":"section","location":"theory.html#References","page":"Theory","text":"[1] GrandPré, Levien, Amir. \"Extremal events dictate population growth rate inference,\" arXiv:2501.08404, 2025\n\n[2] Nemoto, Guevara Hidalgo, Lecomte. \"Finite-time and finite-size scalings in the evaluation of large-deviation functions: Analytical study using a birth-death process,\" Phys. Rev. E 95(1), 2017\n\n[3] Giardinà, Kurchan, Peliti. \"Direct evaluation of large-deviation functions,\" Phys. Rev. Lett. 96(12), 2006\n\n[4] Lecomte & Tailleur. \"A numerical approach to large deviations in continuous time,\" J. Stat. Mech.: Theory Exp. 2007(3), 2007\n\n[5] Amir. \"Cell size regulation in bacteria,\" Phys. Rev. Lett. 112(20), 2014","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Chemostats.jl","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Chemostats.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Introduction","page":"Home","text":"Chemostats.jl is a package to simulate branching populations in Julia, with a focus on cells and bacteria. It implements state-of-the-art algorithms to efficiently estimate population dynamics at sub-exponential cost, based on the paper\n\nKaan Öcal, Lars Maas, Michael P.H. Stumpf. \"Probing bacterial fitness in virtual chemostats.\" bioRxiv (2026).\n\nThis package is somewhat experimental - any feedback is appreciated (either by email or by opening an issue on GitHub).\n\nTo get started, have a look at the Usage section or browse the examples!","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Features","page":"Home","text":"Efficient parallel simulation of branching processes\nDirectly compatible with the SciML ecosystem, including DifferentialEquations.jl, ModelingToolkit.jl and Catalyst.jl\nSupport for basic cell lineage analysis","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#See-also","page":"Home","text":"Chemostats.jl is designed for efficient estimation of growth rates in cell population models. If simple population simulations are required (corresponding to the Direct algorithm), the following packages may be relevant:\n\nAgents.jl: A Julia framework for agent-based modelling.\nAgentBasedModeling.jl: A flexible framework built directly on top of Catalyst. ","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#References","page":"Home","text":"Giardinà, Kurchan, Peliti. \"Direct evaluation of large-deviation functions,\" Phys. Rev. Lett. 96(12), 2006\nLecomte & Tailleur. \"A numerical approach to large deviations in continuous time,\" J. Stat. Mech.: Theory Exp. 2007(3), 2007","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"index.html#Acknowledgments","page":"Home","text":"Thanks to Chris Rackauckas, Aayush Sabharwal, Sam Isaacson, Torkel Loman and the SciML community for their help and support (and their work on the SciML ecosystem!).","title":"Acknowledgments"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/ribosomes.html#Usage","page":"Usage","text":"In this section, we show how to build a simple model of cell growth using Catalyst.jl and Chemostats.jl. In our model, every cell has an exponentially growing volume V, and produces ribosomes R at random, following a jump process. The rate of volume growth and the rate of ribosome production are coupled. The full script is in demo/ribosomes.jl.\n\nusing Catalyst, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5, Chemostats\n\nrn = @reaction_network begin\n    @species R(t) = 0.\n    @variables V(t) = 1.\n    @parameters σ = 10. λ = 1. V_d = 2.\n\n    σ * V, 0 --> R\n\n    @equations begin\n        D(V) ~ λ * R\n    end\nend;","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/ribosomes.html#Division","page":"Usage","text":"To enable division, we have to specify when a cell should divide. We can do this with a DivideCallback that triggers once the cell has reached a fixed division volume V_d:\n\ncb_div = Chemostats.DivideCallback(rn.V ~ rn.V_d);\n\nWe next have to tell Chemostats.jl what happens upon division. Our example is very simple: with probability 1/10, the cell dies; otherwise, each daughter gets half of the volume, and the ribosomes are partitioned binomially:\n\nfunction divide(int)\n    rand() < 0.1 && return nothing\n\n    Chemostats.Offspring(2, :R => Chemostats.BinomialPartition(),\n                            :V => Chemostats.ExactPartition())\nend;","title":"Division"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/ribosomes.html#Simulation","page":"Usage","text":"Each cell is a DECell, which takes a DifferentialEquations.jl problem, a solver, and a divide function. The division callback cb_div is part of the HybridProblem.\n\nu0 = [ :R => 0., :V => 1. ]\np = [ :σ => 10., :λ => 1., :V_d => 2. ]\nprob = HybridProblem(rn, u0, (0., Inf), p; callback = cb_div)\n\ncells = [ DECell(prob, Tsit5(), divide) ];\n\nWe simulate the full population in a Chemostat until time 2, using the Direct algorithm. Since simulate modifies the cells, we pass copy = true to reuse cells below:\n\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(cells, 2., Chemostats.Direct(); copy = true);\n\nWe can then view the cells that are currently alive using chem.pop:\n\nlength(chem.pop)\n\n108\n\n[ cell.int[:V] for cell in first(chem.pop, 5) ]     # volumes of the first five cells\n\n5-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0423164542929497\n 1.016818323022799\n 1.0084091615113993\n 1.0300651269626537\n 1.1075921756578564\n\nWe can also estimate the growth rate of the population using Chemostats.est_Λ:\n\nChemostats.est_Λ(chem)\n\n2.34106561356211\n\nNote that our population grows exponentially, which makes long-term simulations difficult. To avoid this, we can use the Strict or Lax simulation algorithms, which maintain a (roughly) constant population size. For example, we can simulate a population consisting of at most 20 cells for ~100 generations:\n\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(cells, 20., Chemostats.Strict(20, false); copy = true)\nlength(chem.pop), Chemostats.est_Λ(chem)\n\n(20, 2.5697787564815346)\n\nThis allows us to compute much better estimates of population quantities like the growth rate. See Simulation Algorithms for the available algorithms.\n\nChemostats can also record the lineage tree during a simulation, using save_mode = :ancestors or save_mode = :full:\n\nchem = Chemostats.simulate(cells, 20., Chemostats.Strict(20, false); copy = true, save_mode = :ancestors);\n\nThe resulting population tree is stored in chem.tree and can be analysed using the Tree API. See the examples for how to do this.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/trees.html#Tree-API","page":"Tree API","text":"","title":"Tree API"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/trees.html#Trees-and-nodes","page":"Tree API","text":"A Tree stores one Node per cell as a StructVector under tree.nodes. Each node records the parent, generation, birth time and end time of a cell, and a user-defined field data. For living cells, the end time is the time of their last update.\n\nNodes are internally identified by their id and can be accessed as tree[id].  Since tree.nodes is a StructVector, fields can also be accessed for all cells at once, e.g. tree.nodes.t_birth.\n\nCells are stored approximately in order, but some operations require the tree to be exactly sorted, achieved using sort!(tree).\n\nNote: The id of a node is not stable and can change when calling sort! or prune!.","title":"Trees and nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/trees.html#Adding-cells","page":"Tree API","text":"The population tree is modified using the following functions:\n\nFunction Called when\ncell_init!(tree, cells, t) add cells to the population (with no parent)\ncell_divide!(tree, parent, children, t, data) parent divides into children\ncell_die!(tree, cell, t, data) cell dies\ncell_update!(tree, cell, t[, data]) cell is updated to time t","title":"Adding cells"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/trees.html#Pruning-cells","page":"Tree API","text":"For large trees, prune!(tree) performs garbage collection by removing unused branches: only cells that are currently alive or marked using mark! are retained, together with their ancestors.","title":"Pruning cells"},{"category":"section","location":"trees/trees.html#Export","page":"Tree API","text":"Trees can be exported in Newick format, which can be read by many tree-handling packages.","title":"Export"},{"category":"type","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.Tree","page":"Tree API","text":"Tree{C,P=Any}(; min_sweep=1024)\n\nCreates a population tree. Here C is the type of a cell, and P is the type of the data saved for each cell (at division, death or updates), Any if omitted. Each cell is stored in a Node.\n\nmin_sweep is a parameter used for pruning (see prune!).\n\nEvery node in the tree has a unique ID, and nodes can be accessed as tree[id]. IDs are not stable, and can change when the tree is pruned or sorted.\n\nCells are implicitly stored as pairs id => node, and these can be extracted using the functions keys, values and pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.Tree"},{"category":"type","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.Node","page":"Tree API","text":"struct Node{P}\n    parent::Int\n    generation::Int\n    t_birth::Float64\n    t_end::Float64\n    alive::Bool\n    marked::Bool\n    data::Union{Nothing,P}\nend\n\nA node in a Tree, representing a single cell. Roots have parent = 0. For living cells (alive = true), t_end is the time up to which the cell was last updated (see cell_update!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.Node"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/trees.html#Base.sort!-Tuple{Chemostats.Trees.Tree}","page":"Tree API","text":"sort!(tree::Tree)\n\nSorts nodes in a tree. Some functions (e.g. children and descendants) require the tree to be sorted. Does nothing if the tree is already sorted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.sort!"},{"category":"method","location":"trees/trees.html#Base.parent-Tuple{Chemostats.Trees.Tree, Any}","page":"Tree API","text":"parent(tree::Tree, id)\n\nReturns the parent of node id, or 0 if it is a root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parent"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.has_divided","page":"Tree API","text":"has_divided(tree::Tree, id)\n\nReturns true if node id has children in the population tree. Note that pruning may remove unmarked children. Requires a sorted tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.has_divided"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.lifetime","page":"Tree API","text":"lifetime(node::Node)\n\nReturns the lifetime of a node, which is t_end - t_birth. For living cells, returns their age at their last update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.lifetime"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.is_alive","page":"Tree API","text":"is_alive(node::Node)\n\nReturns true if node is a living cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.is_alive"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.cell_init!","page":"Tree API","text":"cell_init!(tree, cells, t)\n\nAdd cells to the population tree at time t (usually called at t = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.cell_init!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.cell_divide!","page":"Tree API","text":"cell_divide!(tree, parent, children, t, data)\n\nCalled when parent divides into children at time t. Here data is the data saved for parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.cell_divide!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.cell_die!","page":"Tree API","text":"cell_die!(tree, cell, t, data)\n\nCalled when cell dies without dividing. Here data is the data saved for cell. A cell that dies at its time of birth is removed from the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.cell_die!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.cell_update!","page":"Tree API","text":"cell_update!(tree, cell, t, data)\ncell_update!(tree, cell, t)\n\nUpdate the cell's current time to t; if data is provided, updates the cell's saved data. Should be called for each living cell before doing tree operations (e.g. at the end of a simulation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.cell_update!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.mark!","page":"Tree API","text":"mark!(tree)\nmark!(tree, ids)\nmark!(tree, nodes)\n\nMarks the nodes ids (or the node collection nodes) to be kept by pruning, together with their ancestors. mark!(tree) marks the current population, i.e. all living cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.mark!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.prune!","page":"Tree API","text":"prune!(tree::Tree; lazy=false)\n\nRemoves all \"dead\" nodes, i.e. nodes that are not ancestors of living cells or of marked nodes (see mark!). If lazy=true, only prunes if the tree has doubled in size since the last pruning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.prune!"},{"category":"function","location":"trees/trees.html#Chemostats.Trees.newick","page":"Tree API","text":"newick(nodes; label = nothing, annotate = nothing)\nnewick(io::IO, nodes; label = nothing, annotate = nothing)\n\nReturns the nodes of the node collection nodes in Newick format. Nodes whose parent is not in nodes are treated as roots. Branch lengths are lifetimes. Requires a sorted tree.\n\nNodes are labelled by their ids, or by label(node) if given. If given, annotate(node) returns a named tuple (or nothing) to be added as an annotation of the form [&key=value,...].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chemostats.Trees.newick"}]
}
